## 3. Security Best Practices

### ✅ DO

- Store tokens ONLY in MMKV/SecureStore (never in Zustand or AsyncStorage for sensitive data)
- Implement token refresh logic in API interceptor
- Clear all auth data on logout (tokens + store)
- Validate and sanitize all user inputs
- Use HTTPS for all API calls
- Implement proper error handling without exposing sensitive info
- Use protected routes for authenticated screens
- Log out users on auth failures (401, 403)

### ❌ DON'T

- Store tokens in state or regular AsyncStorage
- Log sensitive data (tokens, passwords, personal info)
- Trust client-side validation alone
- Expose API keys in code (use environment variables)
- Allow navigation to protected screens without auth check

**Example - Bad:**

```typescript
// ❌ Token in store (security risk)
const useAuthStore = create((set) => ({
  token: null,
  setToken: (token) => set({ token }),
}));

// ❌ Logging sensitive data
console.log('User token:', accessToken);
```

**Example - Good:**

```typescript
// ✅ Token in secure storage
tokenStorage.saveAccessToken(accessToken);

// ✅ Safe logging
console.log('Auth check complete:', isAuthenticated ? 'authenticated' : 'not authenticated');
```

