## 9. Error Handling & UX

### ✅ DO

- Use try-catch for all async operations
- Show user-friendly error messages
- Implement error boundaries for critical sections
- Log errors for debugging (without sensitive data)
- Handle network errors specifically
- Provide fallback UI for errors
- Show loading states for async operations
- Implement optimistic updates where appropriate

### ❌ DON'T

- Let errors crash the app silently
- Show technical error messages to users
- Ignore loading states
- Block UI indefinitely

**Example - Bad:**

```typescript
// ❌ No error handling
async function submitForm() {
  const result = await api.post('/submit', data);
  navigate('/success');
}

// ❌ Technical error to user
catch (error) {
  alert(error.message); // "Cannot read property 'data' of undefined"
}
```

**Example - Good:**

```typescript
// ✅ Proper error handling
async function submitForm() {
  try {
    setLoading(true);
    setError(null);
    const result = await api.post('/submit', data);
    navigate('/success');
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Form submission failed:', error);
    setError('Could not submit form. Please try again.');
  } finally {
    setLoading(false);
  }
}

// ✅ User-friendly message
if (error) {
  return <Text className="text-error">{error}</Text>;
}
```

